Debian README for racoon
------------------------

This package requires a kernel with IPsec (XFRM) support enabled, either
built-in or as loadable modules. The /proc/net/pfkey file must be
available for setkey and racoon to function; if it is not present,
racoon will fail to start.

Make sure all required kernel encryption and xfrm modules are loaded,
or statically linked, before starting racoon.

racoon-tool
-----------
racoon-tool is a Debian-specific management script that simplifies
looking after setkey SPD rules and basic racoon.conf configuration on
a connection-oriented basis. It supports transport mode and tunnel
mode, anonymous VPN service, PSK/X.509 authentication and IPv6.

racoon-tool is not part of upstream racoon. If you need the latest
racoon.conf(5) features or advanced functionality, configure
/etc/racoon/racoon.conf directly instead.

X509 Certificates
------------------

Place the PEM certificate, PEM private key, CA certificate and CA CRL
in /etc/racoon/certs. Racoon requires the CRL file for X.509
certificate verification. Hash names for the CRL and the CA cert must
be set up in that directory with:

# ln -s ca-cacert.pem `openssl x509 -hash -noout -in ca-cacert.pem`.0
# ln -s ca-crl.pem `openssl x509 -hash -noout -in ca-cacert.pem`.r0

The CRL symlink must use the hash of the CA certificate. Certificates
must also have the SubjectAltName X.509v3 attribute set to a DNS name
matching the peer's machine DNS ID. Peer/remote my_identifier and
peers_identifier must be set to the DNS IDs specified in
SubjectAltName, with verify_identifier and verify_cert enabled, and
authentication_method set to rsasig.

This package runs racoon with privilege separation active by default in
both roadwarrior examples (see racoon-admin-guide.html §8.5) -- an
unprivileged, peer-facing process does the actual IKE negotiation, and
is assumed compromised if anything goes wrong. Certificates, the CA
certificate, and the CRL are not secret, but this package's postinst
still keeps /etc/racoon and /etc/racoon/certs at root:racoon 0750 (no
world access at all) rather than the traditional 644/755, since the
unprivileged process opens these files directly and nothing outside
root and the racoon group has any reason to. When you add your own
certificate/CRL files under /etc/racoon/certs, match that policy:

# chmod 640 *cert.pem *crl.pem
# chown root:racoon *cert.pem *crl.pem

Private keys are read exclusively by the privileged process (never by
the unprivileged, peer-facing one), so they stay root:root 600:

# chmod 600 *key.pem
# chown root:root *key.pem

Get this wrong and the failure mode differs by file. For
/etc/racoon/psk.txt specifically, racoon enforces it at load time
(safefile(), src/racoon/safefile.c): the file must be owned by the
loading process's own UID with no group or world permission bits set
at all, or racoon logs "has weak file permission"/"has invalid owner
uid" and refuses to load it -- there is no silent degraded mode for
psk.txt. Certificate and private-key file permissions above are not
independently checked by racoon the same way; loosening those does not
make racoon refuse to start, it just quietly defeats the reason
privilege separation exists in the first place.

Once installed, configure the key and certificate with the
certificate_type x509 statement in racoon.conf, or certificate_type
in racoon-tool.conf.

iPhone OS / iOS Support
------------------------
Historical context (L2TP/IPsec with pre-shared keys):
Older iOS versions and the native "L2TP" VPN type only support Main
Mode with pre-shared keys (PSK). Since L2TP provides per-user
authentication, administrators often want a single PSK for all
roadwarrior clients. racoon traditionally requires PSK lookup by IP
address or identity; to support roaming clients whose IP is unknown,
ipsec-tools added a wildcard match in psk.txt. Specifying '*' in the
ID field matches any client identity. This is needed only for legacy
L2TP/IPsec with PSKs.

Modern usage (IKEv2 or IPsec with certificates):
Current iOS versions fully support IKEv2 and IPsec profiles using
X.509 client certificates. With certificate-based authentication the
wildcard PSK workaround is not required; racoon validates clients via
their certificate chain and Subject DN/AltName, and the wildcard
entry in psk.txt is ignored when certificate authentication is
active.

Plainrsa to PEM file format converter
---------------------------------------

A Perl script to convert the OpenBSD key file format to the one used
by racoon, plainrsa-convert, is installed in
/usr/share/doc/racoon/examples. It requires the
libparse-recdescent-perl and libcrypt-openssl-rsa-perl packages.

Road-warrior split-DNS and routing hooks
------------------------------------------

This package ships phase1-up/phase1-down script hooks for Mode-Config
road-warrior clients (racoon-hook-lib.sh, phase1-up.sh, phase1-down.sh,
and the racoon-dns-detect diagnostic CLI), built from
src/racoon/scripts/ and installed to /etc/racoon/scripts/ as a real
part of this package -- not a manual copy-in. A commented sample
config, hooks.conf.sample, ships alongside as a
/usr/share/doc/racoon/examples entry (dh_installexamples); copy it to
/etc/racoon/hooks.conf if you want to override any of its defaults.

None of the resolver tools the hooks shell out to at runtime
(iproute2, and optionally one of
network-manager/systemd-resolved/resolvconf/dnsmasq depending on which
backend a given install actually uses) are hard package dependencies:
each is probed at runtime and only the backends actually present are
used, so nothing is pulled in unconditionally by installing this
package.

To use them, reference the installed phase1-up.sh/phase1-down.sh paths
from the `script` directives in your racoon.conf:

  remote anyremote
  {
      ...
      script "/etc/racoon/scripts/phase1-up.sh" phase1_up;
      script "/etc/racoon/scripts/phase1-down.sh" phase1_down;
  }

See /usr/share/doc/racoon/split-dns.html (doc/admin/split-dns.html in
the source tree) for full usage, configuration, and troubleshooting
documentation, including the diagnostic CLI:

  /etc/racoon/scripts/racoon-dns-detect --detect --explain
